        "text": "return self.i_size\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\ncar = Car (\"blue\", 1000, \"2.5L\")\nprint(car)\nprint( car.i_engine.i_size )\nUnderstanding OOP principles     87\nIn this example, we defined an Engine inner class inside our regular Car class. The\nEngine class has only one attribute\u2014 i_size , the constructor method ( __init__) ,\nand the __str__ method. For the Car class, we updated the following as compared to\nour previous examples:\n\u2022I The __init__ method includes a new attribute for engine size, and a new line has\nbeen added to create a new instance of Engine associated with the Car instance.\n\u2022I The __str__ method of the Car class includes the i_size inner class attributes\nin it.\nThe main program is using a print statement on the Car instance and also has a line to\nprint the value of the i_size attribute of the Engine class. The console output of this\nprogram will be similar to what is shown here:\ncar with color blue, mileage 1000 and engine of 2.5L",
